From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michael Bommarito <michael.bommarito@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 21 Apr 2026 11:34:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] kernel: mark kthread() and smpboot_thread_fn() notrace

Both functions end in do_exit() rather than a normal return.
The generic function_graph tracer pushes a shadow-stack entry
at their entry expecting a matching pop on return; that pop
never fires, leaving a stale entry on current->ret_stack.

On architectures whose task-switch mechanism always unwinds
through the frame pointer (including x86_64 with CONFIG_HAVE_
FUNCTION_GRAPH_RETVAL=y in the stock configuration), the stale
entry is harmless: the ret_stack walk never dereferences the
leaked retp because real task teardown goes through do_exit()
→ do_task_dead() → schedule() without touching ret_stack.

On architectures whose task resume uses setjmp/longjmp-style
mechanisms (so a graphed function's ret pops a shadow entry
that may not correspond to the host stack the task was
resumed into), the stale pointer can be followed as a return
address. Observed: SEGV in schedule+0x4a with RAX=0 and
return_to_handler present twice in the stack trace, the second
occurrence coming from a ret that consumed the leaked entry.

The narrow fix is to skip graph tracing at these two specific
generic-kernel entry points. User-written threadfns that
kthread() eventually calls are still graph-traceable; only the
wrappers themselves are excluded. Matches the discipline
already applied to ret_from_fork()-adjacent assembly stubs in
arch/x86/entry.

No functional impact on architectures where the leak was
already harmless — notrace here only suppresses push/pop at
these two call sites, which already don't need the graph event
for trace analysis (the functions exist to be the landing pad
for a threadfn, not to be themselves the subject of trace
analysis).

Signed-off-by: Michael Bommarito <michael.bommarito@gmail.com>
---
 kernel/kthread.c  | 22 +++++++++++++++++++++-
 kernel/smpboot.c  | 11 ++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 31 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/kernel/kthread.c b/kernel/kthread.c
index 791210daf8b4..0243c371dbe3 100644
--- a/kernel/kthread.c
+++ b/kernel/kthread.c
@@ -377,7 +377,27 @@ static void kthread_affine_node(void)
 	free_cpumask_var(affinity);
 }

-static int kthread(void *_create)
+/*
+ * notrace: the generic function_graph tracer pushes a shadow-
+ * stack entry at kthread() entry, expecting a matching pop on
+ * return. kthread() does not return — it ends in do_exit().
+ * On architectures whose task resume always goes through the
+ * frame pointer, the leaked entry is harmless (the ret_stack
+ * walk tolerates stale entries because real task teardown
+ * doesn't touch ret_stack). On architectures whose task
+ * resume uses setjmp/longjmp-style mechanisms the leaked
+ * pointer can be followed as a return address when a graphed
+ * function on the unwind path pops through the shadow stack.
+ *
+ * The narrow fix is to skip graph tracing at this specific
+ * entry. Any user-written kthread threadfn is still
+ * graph-traced; only the generic kthread()-the-wrapper is
+ * skipped.
+ *
+ * Same rationale applies to smpboot_thread_fn(); see
+ * kernel/smpboot.c.
+ */
+static notrace int kthread(void *_create)
 {
 	static const struct sched_param param = { .sched_priority = 0 };
 	/* Copy data: it's on kthread's stack */
diff --git a/kernel/smpboot.c b/kernel/smpboot.c
index 4503b60ce9bd..1b85a27103c8 100644
--- a/kernel/smpboot.c
+++ b/kernel/smpboot.c
@@ -99,7 +99,16 @@ enum {
  *
  * Returns 1 when the thread should exit, 0 otherwise.
  */
-static int smpboot_thread_fn(void *data)
+/*
+ * notrace: reached via kthread_create_on_cpu() and entered from
+ * kthread() as the threadfn. Both entry points end in
+ * do_exit() rather than a normal return. The generic
+ * function_graph tracer's shadow-stack push at this entry is
+ * never matched by a pop on return; on architectures whose
+ * task resume doesn't always unwind through the frame pointer
+ * the leaked entry can be followed. See kernel/kthread.c for
+ * the full rationale; same shape applies here.
+ */
+static notrace int smpboot_thread_fn(void *data)
 {
 	struct task_struct *td = current;

--
